Requirements:
-------------

1. Xcode (for the compiler, SDK, and signing/notarization tools)
2. CMake and Ninja (e.g. `brew install cmake ninja`)
3. Qt 5 or 6.  For development, Homebrew, vcpkg, or the official Qt installer all work.  For
   a release, use the official Qt installer's `macos` build, whose frameworks are universal
   (x86_64 + arm64); Homebrew's Qt is single-architecture.

The minimum macOS version Quackle supports is taken from the Qt it's built against (e.g. Qt
6.8 requires macOS 12).  CMake prints it at configure time ("macOS deployment target"), and
it is applied to the compiler and to `LSMinimumSystemVersion` in the app's Info.plist.  Pass
`-DCMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=<version>` only to raise it.


Building Quackle:
-----------------

Follow the cmake instructions in [README.md](README.md).  You can use the Xcode generator
to make an Xcode project if you wish.  That builds the raw `Quackle` executable, which runs
against the data/ directory in the source tree.


Building the app bundle:
------------------------

To build a Quackle.app bundle instead of the raw executable, configure with `-DBUNDLE=ON`.
This builds the .app, sets its Info.plist and icon, and copies the data/ directory
(alphabets, lexica, strategy, themes) into Contents/Resources/data so the bundled app is
self-contained.  From quackle/quacker/:

	mkdir build && cd build
	cmake -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<path_to_qt> -DBUNDLE=ON ..
	cmake --build .

`<path_to_qt>` is your Qt prefix, e.g. ~/Qt/6.8.3/macos or the Homebrew/vcpkg prefix.
If you use the Xcode generator, add `--config Release` (or Debug) to the build command.

At this point Quackle.app runs locally, but it still links against the Qt frameworks in
your Qt install, so it won't run on a machine without Qt.  The `macdeploy` target (see
below) copies Qt into the bundle.


Making a release
================

A release is a universal, Developer ID-signed, notarized Quackle.app inside a signed,
notarized, stapled .dmg, distributed outside the Mac App Store.  It's built as
RelWithDebInfo, and its debug info (a .dSYM) is archived separately so crash reports from the
field can be symbolicated.

Prerequisites
-------------

Releasing assumes the following are already set up on the Mac you release from.  Check each
before starting.

1. **A Developer ID Application certificate and its private key**, in the login keychain,
   along with Apple's "Developer ID - G2" intermediate certificate
   (https://www.apple.com/certificateauthority/).  This should report one valid identity
   named "Developer ID Application: <Name> (<TEAMID>)"; that exact string is the signing
   identity used below.

		security find-identity -v -p codesigning

2. **Notarization credentials** for the developer account, stored in the keychain by
   `xcrun notarytool store-credentials` under the profile name `quackle-notary`.  This
   should list past submissions (possibly none) without an error:

		xcrun notarytool history --keychain-profile quackle-notary

3. **A universal Qt** (the official installer's `macos` build).  This should list
   `x86_64 arm64`:

		lipo -info <path to Qt>/macos/lib/QtCore.framework/QtCore

4. **create-dmg** (https://github.com/create-dmg/create-dmg, e.g. via Homebrew).  This
   should print a version:

		create-dmg --version


Release procedure
-----------------

Run everything from the repository root unless noted.

1. **Set shell variables** for the rest of the procedure, in the terminal you'll use:

		export QT_PREFIX=<path to Qt>/macos
		export IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: <Name> (<TEAMID>)"
		export VERSION=x.y.z
		export DMG=quackle-$VERSION-macos.dmg
		export DSYM_ZIP=quackle-$VERSION-macos-dSYM.zip

2. **Configure** in an empty build directory:

		rm -rf build.release && mkdir build.release && cd build.release
		cmake --fresh -GNinja \
			-DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$QT_PREFIX" \
			-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
			-DCMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES="x86_64;arm64" \
			-DBUNDLE=ON \
			-DQUACKLE_CODESIGN_IDENTITY="$IDENTITY" \
			../quacker/

   In the output, check that "Qt:" shows the intended Qt, "macOS deployment target" is the
   minimum you expect, and the sanitizers report "none enabled".

3. **Build, extract debug info, then deploy and sign.**  `dsymutil` must run before
   `macdeploy`: `macdeployqt` strips the executable's debug map, after which the debug info
   can no longer be collected.  `macdeploy` copies the Qt frameworks and plugins into the
   bundle and signs everything with a hardened runtime and secure timestamp (both required
   for notarization).  It needs network access for the timestamp.

		ninja
		dsymutil Quackle.app/Contents/MacOS/Quackle -o Quackle.app.dSYM
		ninja macdeploy

   Plain `ninja` also builds the regression-test tools used in step 5.  If anything causes
   Quackle to relink after `dsymutil`, run `dsymutil` again; the check in step 4 catches
   this.

4. **Check the bundle** (still in build.release):

		lipo -info Quackle.app/Contents/MacOS/Quackle

   should list `x86_64 arm64`.

		dwarfdump --uuid Quackle.app/Contents/MacOS/Quackle Quackle.app.dSYM

   should show the same UUID for the app and the dSYM, for each architecture.  A dSYM
   only symbolicates the exact build it came from.

		vtool -show-build Quackle.app/Contents/MacOS/Quackle | grep minos

   should show the deployment target, once per architecture.

		codesign --verify --deep --strict --verbose=2 Quackle.app
		codesign -dvv Quackle.app 2>&1 | grep -E 'Authority|flags|Timestamp'

   should report "valid on disk" and "satisfies its Designated Requirement"; the second
   should show `Authority=Developer ID Application: ...`, `flags=0x10000(runtime)`, and a
   `Timestamp=` line (not "Signed Time").

		find Quackle.app/Contents/MacOS Quackle.app/Contents/Frameworks Quackle.app/Contents/PlugIns \
			-type f -perm -u+x | xargs otool -L | \
			grep -v -E ':$|@rpath/|@executable_path/|@loader_path/|/System/Library/|/usr/lib/'

   should print nothing.  Any output is a library linked from outside the bundle (e.g. your
   Qt install), which won't exist on users' Macs.

5. **Run the regression suite** against this build (from the repository root); it must pass:

		cd ..
		python3 test/regression/run_regression.py --bin build.release
		cd build.release

6. **Smoke test.**  `open Quackle.app`: check that Help > About shows the new version, start
   a game against a computer player, and run a simulation.

7. **Create the DMG.**  create-dmg packs the *contents* of a folder, so stage the app in
   its own folder.  The window size matches DmgBackground.png (500x250).  The first time,
   macOS asks to let your terminal control Finder (used to lay out the window); allow it.

		rm -rf dmg-staging "$DMG" && mkdir dmg-staging
		ditto Quackle.app dmg-staging/Quackle.app
		create-dmg --volname Quackle --window-size 500 250 \
			--background ../DmgBackground.png \
			--icon Quackle.app 100 100 --app-drop-link 400 100 \
			"$DMG" dmg-staging

8. **Sign the DMG:**

		codesign -s "$IDENTITY" --timestamp "$DMG"

9. **Notarize.**  This uploads the DMG; Apple checks the DMG and everything in it.  It
   usually takes a few minutes.

		xcrun notarytool submit "$DMG" --keychain-profile quackle-notary --wait

   The result should be `status: Accepted`.  If it's `Invalid`, get the reasons with

		xcrun notarytool log <submission-id> --keychain-profile quackle-notary

   fix the problem, and start over from step 2.

10. **Staple** the notarization ticket to the DMG, so Gatekeeper can verify it offline,
    and check the result:

		xcrun stapler staple "$DMG"
		xcrun stapler validate "$DMG"
		spctl -a -t open --context context:primary-signature -v "$DMG"

    The last should say `accepted` and `source=Notarized Developer ID`.

11. **Archive the debug info.**  It isn't shipped to users; keep it with the release's
    records, since no other build (even from the same source) will match crash reports
    from this one.

		ditto -c -k --keepParent Quackle.app.dSYM "$DSYM_ZIP"


Symbolicating crash reports
---------------------------

Unzip the archived dSYM for the release.  In a macOS crash report, the "Binary Images"
section (`usedImages` in a raw .ips file) gives Quackle's load address and UUID; check that
the UUID matches `dwarfdump --uuid` on the dSYM.  Then pass the load address and the frame
addresses from the backtrace to `atos`, using the architecture from the report:

	atos -o Quackle.app.dSYM/Contents/Resources/DWARF/Quackle -arch arm64 \
		-l <load address> <address> [<address> ...]

Each address resolves to a function, source file, and line.  Frames inside the Qt
frameworks resolve only to exported symbol names.
